  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe potential exists for multiple rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms into Thursday as a slow moving and eventually quasi \nstationary boundary sets up across the area. This leads to an \noverall low confidence forecast and a more initially conservative \napproach, especially in later periods. \n\nIn the near term, scattered showers and storms have continued to \ndevelop and move NE across far NW areas with some indications on \nsatellite development may calm down in this area as the frontal \nboundary shifts northeast. Low level moisture is increasing with an \naxis of 500 to 1000 J/KG of MUCAPE into SW areas ahead of a remnant \nboundary/trough residing from central Lower MI to near Chicago and \nthen to Quincy IL. This will most likely be the focus for additional \nshowers and storms as we move through the remainder of the\nafternoon into this evening with locations closer to Lake MI \nseeing activity first. The best shear will remain disconnected \nfrom this boundary and best upper dynamics will push NE leaving \nlittle for storms to work with in terms of organization. Combine\nthat with decreasing, but not zero, instability after sunset \nleads to low confidence on how long any convection survives as \nwell as risk for renewed development. While I suspect even \nlikely pops may be overdone into this evening, will leave them \nin place during the most favorable window when a weak waves does\nmove in. Although the marginal risk for severe continues (wind \nthreat), this may very well be more of a potential hydro issue \nwith time. Initially, much of the area can take some rainfall, \nbut if any training occurs this could quickly change as PWATs \nover near 2\" which could result on 1 to maybe 2 inch per hour \nrates. \n\nBeyond tonight, consensus of surrounding offices was generally to \nback away from any pops higher than chc given the lack of \nconsistency in the models, difficulty in timing specific waves and \nlocation of the boundary. Combination of soupy airmass (dewpoints \nupper 60s to low 70s), generally uncapped atmosphere (assuming no \nconvective inhibition from other storm complexes) and pockets of \nheating will lead to mainly diurnally driven development outside of \nany MCVs or waves. One item to note is the SW to NW flow currently \nin place will transition to more west east as the upper flow \nflattens out. The chances for localized flooding may then increase \nsomewhat given the weak flow and risk for training. WPC keeps much \nof the area in a Marginal Risk for Excessive rainfall, with Wed \ndepicting Slgt chc into parts of the area along the forecasted \nlocation of the front. \n\nForecast left untouched beyond Thursday as the frontal boundary will \nlikely osculate across the area with 500 mb heights of 594 to 597 \ndm across the area which may be enough to push the boundary somewhat \nfurther north. However, as we get to the weekend another trough will \nslowly edge east and reach the western Dakotas Sunday with a series \nof weak disturbances helping to displace the ridge south and \nhopefully bring a break in the humidity. Highest pops currently \nreside Sat/Sat night and may be overdone (likely to some \ncategorical) but that far out no changes warranted.",
